'''Pierre Jean-Jacques Gasly''' ({{IPA|fr|pjɛʁ ɡasli}}; born 7 February 1996) is a French [[racing driver]] who competes in [[Formula One]] for [[Alpine F1 Team|Alpine]]. Gasly won the [[2020 Italian Grand Prix]] with [[Scuderia AlphaTauri|AlphaTauri]].

Born and raised in [[Normandy]], Gasly began competitive [[kart racing]] aged 10. Graduating to [[junior formulae]] in 2011, Gasly finished third in the [[2011 French F4 Championship|French F4 Championship]] that year with the [[FFSA Academy]]. He won his first championship at the [[Formula Renault Eurocup]] in [[2013 Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0|2013]] with [[Tech 1 Racing|Tech 1]]. Gasly then progressed to the [[GP2 Series]], winning the championship in [[2016 GP2 Series|2016]] with [[Prema Racing|Prema]] amid a close title battle with [[Antonio Giovinazzi]]. He moved to [[Super Formula]] the [[2017 Super Formula Championship|following year]], finishing runner-up to [[Hiroaki Ishiura]] with [[Team Mugen|Mugen]], and debuted in [[Formula E]] at the [[2017 New York City ePrix|New York City ePrix]] for [[Renault e.dams]].

A member of the [[Red Bull Junior Team]] since 2014, Gasly made his Formula One debut with [[Toro Rosso]] at the [[2017 Malaysian Grand Prix]], replacing [[Daniil Kvyat]] for the remainder of the {{F1|2017}} season. He completed a full-time move to Toro Rosso for {{F1|2018}}, before being promoted to parent team [[Red Bull Racing|Red Bull]] in {{F1|2019}}. Gasly was demoted after the {{F1GP|2019|Hungarian}}, replaced by [[Alexander Albon]]; he achieved his maiden podium at the {{F1GP|2019|Brazilian}} that year with Toro Rosso. In {{F1|2020}}, Gasly took his maiden [[List of Formula One Grand Prix winners|victory]] at the {{F1GP|2020|Italian}} whilst driving for the re-branded [[Scuderia AlphaTauri|AlphaTauri]]. He achieved a third podium for the team at the {{F1GP|2021|Azerbaijan}} in {{F1|2021}}. Gasly moved to [[Alpine F1 Team|Alpine]] for his {{F1|2023}} campaign, scoring his first podium for the team at the {{F1GP|2023|Dutch}}, repeating this feat at the rain-affected {{F1GP|2024|São Paulo}} in {{F1|2024}} and the {{F1GP|2026|Monaco}} in {{F1|2026}}.

As of the {{Latest F1GP}}, Gasly has achieved {{Numbertoword|{{F1stat|GAS|wins}}}} race win, {{Numbertoword|{{F1stat|GAS|fastestlaps}}}} fastest laps and {{Numbertoword|{{F1stat|GAS|podiums}}}} podiums in Formula One. Gasly is contracted to remain at Alpine until at least the end of the 2028 season.

== Early and personal life ==
Pierre Jean-Jacques Gasly was born on 7 February 1996 in [[Rouen]], France, to father Jean-Jacques Gasly and mother Pascale.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/11194456/filing-history |title=THE GRID AGENCY LTD |author=<!--Not stated--> |website=Companies House |access-date=20 January 2023}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=archyde|date=8 September 2020|title=Pierre Gasly: "I dreamed of victory a thousand times while watching Schumacher"|url=https://www.archyde.com/pierre-gasly-%e2%80%8b%e2%80%8bi-dreamed-of-victory-a-thousand-times-while-watching-schumacher/|access-date=31 December 2020|website=Archyde|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Mums of the 2020 F1 drivers - YouTube|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bBmPNNAtxOg&ab_channel=KymIllman|access-date=31 December 2020|website=www.youtube.com| date=8 October 2020 }}</ref> Gasly is the youngest of five and has four [[half brother]]s: two [[maternal]] from his mother's previous marriage, Nicolas Caron and Cyril Caron; and two [[paternal]] from his father's previous marriage, Phillipe Gasly and Paul Gasly.<ref>{{Cite web |title=MEET THE FAMILIES BEHIND THESE F1 DRIVERS - YouTube |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bhytHLP_ih8&t=48s&ab_channel=KymIllman |access-date=31 December 2020 |website=www.youtube.com| date=28 January 2020 }}</ref><ref name="motorsportweek.com">{{Cite web |date=13 March 2018 |title=Interview: Pierre Gasly ready to step on the gas |url=https://www.motorsportweek.com/2018/03/13/17271/ |access-date=31 December 2020 |website=Motorsport Week |language=en-GB}}</ref> Gasly was raised Christian and routinely makes the sign of the cross to bless himself before races.<ref>{{cite web |last=Sunderland |first=Tom |date=21 December 2022 |title=FIA face Pierre Gasly headache as new F1 rules cast uncertainty over his pre-race ritual |url=https://www.express.co.uk/sport/f1-autosport/1712459/FIA-Pierre-Gasly-headache-new-F1-rules-prehttps://www.express.co.uk/sport/f1-autosport/1712459/FIA-Pierre-Gasly-headache-new-F1-rules-pre-race-ritual-race-ritual |access-date=12 April 2025 |website=[[Daily Express]] }}{{Dead link|date=April 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref>{{Better source needed|reason=WP:DAILYEXPRESS|date=April 2025}}

Gasly's family has long been involved in [[motorsport]]s. His grandfather competed in [[Kart racing|karting]], his grandmother a kart champion, and his father Jean Jacques has also competed in various categories of racing including [[Kart racing|karting]], [[Endurance racing (motorsport)|endurance racing]] and [[rallying]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=PIERRE'S BIOGRAPHY|url=https://www.scuderiaalphatauri.com/en/pierres-biography/|access-date=31 December 2020|website=SCUDERIA ALPHATAURI|date=13 September 2017 |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite magazine |last=Coleman |first=Madeline |date=10 March 2022 |title=Pierre Gasly's Remarkable Rise to F1—and Netflix—Stardom |url=https://www.si.com/racing/2022/03/10/pierre-gasly-formula-one-netflix-drive-to-survive |access-date=20 March 2022 |magazine=Sports Illustrated |language=en}}</ref> His father stopped rallying when he 'fell off [a] mountain' after his co-driver made a mistake reading the pace note.<ref name=":0" /> At the age of six, Gasly first experienced karting at a local karting track in [[Anneville-Ambourville]].<ref name="motorsportweek.com" /> He was 13 years old when he left Rouen for Le Mans after his commitment for competitive racing.<ref name=":0" />

Gasly grew up alongside [[Anthoine Hubert]]; karting with him since the age of seven, being educated at the same private school and having resided together as [[roommate]]s for several years.<ref>{{Cite web|date=2 September 2019|title="You're Not Ready at 22 to Lose Your Best Mate" - Gasly Gets Emotional After Loss of Childhood Friend Anthoine Hubert|url=https://www.essentiallysports.com/pierre-gasly-opens-up-on-his-emotions-after-tragic-passing-of-anthoine-hubert/|access-date=31 December 2020|website=EssentiallySports|language=en-US}}</ref> He has been close friends with [[Charles Leclerc]] and [[Esteban Ocon]]<ref>{{Cite web|last=Chokhani|first=Darshan|date=5 October 2018|title=Gasly discusses broken friendship with Ocon on F1 podcast, help from Vettel|url=https://drivetribe.com/p/gasly-discusses-broken-friendship-fHdDTFoeTOmGMdUTwNf4ZQ|access-date=28 June 2021|website=DriveTribe|language=en|archive-date=28 June 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210628050529/https://drivetribe.com/p/gasly-discusses-broken-friendship-fHdDTFoeTOmGMdUTwNf4ZQ|url-status=dead}}</ref> since a young age. However, his relationship with Ocon deteriorated during their karting career.<ref>{{Cite web|date=5 April 2018|title=Gasly explains Ocon rift: "I started to beat him, he didn't like it"|url=https://www.racefans.net/2018/04/05/gasly-explains-ocon-rift-i-started-beat-didnt-like/|access-date=22 January 2023|website=RaceFans|language=en}}</ref> In 2019 he moved to [[Milan]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=6 October 2019|title=How Toro Rosso's Italian family spirit can give Gasly stability|url=https://www.crash.net/f1/feature/930947/1/how-toro-rosso-s-italian-family-spirit-can-give-gasly-stability|access-date=28 June 2021|website=Crash|language=en}}</ref> Besides his native French, Gasly also speaks Italian<ref>{{cite tweet|number=968115534498877440|user=AlphaTauriF1|title= He can speak Italian better than us! Welcome to Faenza Pierre! 🤭 |date=26 February 2018}}</ref> and English.

Gasly has been dating Francisca Gomes since October 2022. Gomes is a model and influencer and she is signed to the Portugal-based modeling agency Central Models. The couple went official in the start of 2023.<ref>{{cite web |title=Who Is Pierre Gasly's Girlfriend? All About Francisca Gomes |url=https://people.com/who-is-francisca-gomes-pierre-gasly-7510289 |website=People.com |access-date=1 October 2024 |language=en}}</ref>

== Junior racing career ==
===Karting===
Gasly entered competitive [[karting]] in 2006 at the age of ten, when he finished fifteenth in the French Minime Championship,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.driverdb.com/standings/1247-2006/|title=Championnat de France&nbsp;– Minime 2006|work=driverdb.com|publisher=Driver Database|access-date=20 October 2013}}</ref> before he finished fourth the following year.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.driverdb.com/standings/1247-2007/|title=Championnat de France&nbsp;– Minime 2007|work=driverdb.com|publisher=Driver Database|access-date=20 October 2013}}</ref> In 2008, he stepped up to the French Cadet Championship,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.driverdb.com/standings/2456-2008/|title=Championnat de France&nbsp;– Cadet 2008|work=driverdb.com|publisher=Driver Database|access-date=20 October 2013}}</ref> before moving to the international scene in 2009. He moved into the [[KF3]] category, staying until the end of 2010, when he finished as runner-up in the [[CIK-FIA Karting European Championship|CIK-FIA European Championship]].<ref name="Lead">{{cite news|url=http://www.paddockscout.com/leading-the-new-french-generation-pierre-gasly/|title=Leading the new French generation: Pierre Gasly|last=David|first=Gruz|date=20 September 2013|publisher=Paddock Scout|access-date=20 October 2013}}</ref>

===Formula Renault===
[[File:Pierre Gasly en Motorland.JPG|thumb|left|Pierre Gasly in [[Motorland Aragón]], 2014]]
In [[2011 French F4 Championship season|2011]], Gasly made his début in [[Open-wheel car|single-seaters]], taking part in the [[French F4 Championship]] 1.6-litre category.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.autosportacademy.com/gb/competition/2011-season/drivers/p/37/r/|title=Drivers 2011|access-date=29 October 2011|work=Autosport Academy|publisher=[[French F4 Championship]]|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111003140237/http://www.autosportacademy.com/gb/competition/2011-season/drivers/p/37/r/|archive-date=3 October 2011}}</ref> He finished third behind his future Eurocup rivals [[Matthieu Vaxivière]] and Andrea Pizzitola with seven podiums, including wins at [[Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps|Spa]], [[Circuit d'Albi|Albi]] and [[Circuit Paul Ricard|Le Castellet]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.paddockscout.com/weekend-review-bortolotti-closes-f2-season-in-dominant-fashion/|title=Weekend Review: Bortolotti closes F2 season in dominant fashion|last=Allen|first=Peter|date=20 October 2013|publisher=Paddock Scout|access-date=20 October 2013|quote=Pierre Gasly won both races, giving him third in the final standings behind Andrea Pizzitola.}}</ref>

Gasly moved to the 2-litre Formula Renault machinery in 2012, joining R-Ace GP in the [[2012 Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0 season|Formula Renault Eurocup]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.renaultsport.com/New-boys-and-old-hands-raring-to.html|title=New boys and old hands raring to go|date=27 April 2012|work=[[Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0]]|publisher=[[Renault Sport]]|access-date=20 October 2013|archive-date=23 October 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131023022242/http://www.renaultsport.com/New-boys-and-old-hands-raring-to.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> He finished tenth with six point-scoring finishes, including podiums at Spa and the [[Nürburgring]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.paddockscout.com/13-drivers-to-watch-in-2013/|title=13 drivers to watch in 2013|last=Allen|first=Peter|date=11 January 2013|publisher=Paddock Scout|access-date=20 October 2013}}</ref> He also had seven starts in the [[2012 Formula Renault 2.0 NEC season|Formula Renault 2.0 Northern European Cup]] with the same team, taking a podium at the Nürburgring.

For [[2013 Eurocup Formula Renault 2.0 season|2013]], Gasly moved to [[Tech 1 Racing]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.paddockscout.com/gasly-switches-to-tech-1-for-second-year-in-the-eurocup/|title=Gasly switches to Tech 1 for second year in the Eurocup|last=Khorounzhiy|first=Valentin|date=29 January 2013|publisher=Paddock Scout|access-date=30 January 2013}}</ref> He took five podiums, as well as victories at [[Moscow Raceway|Moscow]], the [[Hungaroring]] and Le Castellet.<ref name="Lead"/><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.paddockscout.com/gasly-takes-championship-lead-with-controlled-race-one-victory/|title=Gasly takes championship lead with controlled race one victory|last=Musker|first=Ant|date=20 October 2013|publisher=Paddock Scout|access-date=20 October 2013}}</ref> He held an eleven-point lead over [[Oliver Rowland]] into the final meeting at [[Circuit de Barcelona-Catalunya]], and ultimately clinched the title with third and sixth-place finishes; the latter result coming after a collision with Rowland, who received a drive-through penalty as a result.<ref name="Eurocup">{{cite news|url=http://www.paddockscout.com/gasly-crowned-champion-as-ocon-wins-final-race/|title=Gasly crowned champion as Ocon wins final race|last=Musker|first=Ant|date=20 October 2013|publisher=Paddock Scout|access-date=20 October 2013}}</ref>

Gasly jumped to [[Formula Renault 3.5 Series]] in 2014, where he was hired by [[Arden Motorsport|Arden]] under the [[Red Bull Junior Team]] development program. He finished the season as runner-up to another Red Bull Junior [[Carlos Sainz Jr.]], collecting eight podiums in the seventeen races.

===GP2 Series===
[[File:2014 Italian GP2 round (14968955090).jpg|thumb|Gasly racing at the [[2014 Monza GP2 Series round]]]]
Gasly made his [[GP2 Series]] debut in 2014 at [[Autodromo Nazionale Monza|Monza]] circuit in support of [[2014 Italian Grand Prix|Italian Grand Prix]], replacing [[Caterham Racing (GP2 team)|Caterham Racing]] driver [[Tom Dillmann]] who had commitments at other racing series and was unable to participate in GP2 Series races for that weekend.<ref name="Gasly">{{cite news|url=http://www.italiaracing.net/notizia.asp?id=48008&cat=4|title=Gasly debutta a Monza con Caterham|last=Costa|first=Massimo|date=31 August 2014|work=ItaliaRacing.net|publisher=Inpagina|language=it|access-date=7 September 2014|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140907074158/http://www.italiaracing.net/notizia.asp?id=48008&cat=4|archive-date=7 September 2014}}</ref> He then partook in post-season testing, driving for [[DAMS]] and signed with the French team to race alongside the British driver [[Alex Lynn]], development driver of [[Williams F1 Team]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://callumrowesgp2blog.com/2015/01/08/gasly-lynn-confirmed-as-dams-drivers-for-2015/ |title=Gasly, Lynn confirmed as DAMS drivers for 2015 &#124; Callum Rowe's GP2 Blog |access-date=8 January 2015 |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150108150715/http://callumrowesgp2blog.com/2015/01/08/gasly-lynn-confirmed-as-dams-drivers-for-2015/ |archive-date=8 January 2015 }}</ref> Though taking three pole positions and four podiums, Gasly experienced an uneven season, including causing collisions in [[Bahrain International Circuit|Bahrain]], [[Circuit de Spa-Francorchamps|Spa]] and [[Yas Marina Circuit|Yas Marina]] (which got the subsequent race cancelled), which saw him finish eighth, two places behind teammate Lynn.

Gasly switched to newcomers [[Prema Powerteam]] alongside [[2015 European Formula 3 season|2015 European Formula 3]] runner-up and GP2 rookie [[Antonio Giovinazzi]] for [[2016 GP2 Series|2016]]. Pressured by Red Bull Junior Team boss [[Helmut Marko]] to win the title or be dropped from the programme — with Red Bull reducing their financial contribution to $500,000, half the total needed to pay for a GP2 seat at an established frontrunning team — Gasly went on to become the GP2 Series champion that season.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kalinauckas |first=Alex |date=2026-05-03 |title=Pierre Gasly’s desperate trip to Uganda to fund an F1 dream: ‘I’m not even sure he exists’ |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7215965/2026/04/23/pierre-gasly-uganda-sponsor-f1/ |access-date=2026-05-08 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

==Formula One career==
In December 2013, it was announced that Gasly would be inducted into the [[Red Bull Junior Team]] for the [[2014 Formula One season]] alongside future GP2 Series teammate [[Alex Lynn]] and future [[Scuderia Toro Rosso]] teammate [[Carlos Sainz Jr.]]<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.redbull.com/gb-en/2014-red-bull-junior-team-announcement|title=Red Bull Junior Team announces 2014 trio|website=redbull.com|first=Tom|last=Bellingham|date=18 December 2013|access-date=24 June 2021}}</ref> Gasly's first experience in Formula One machinery came in May 2015 at the in-season test at the [[Circuit de Barcelona-Catalunya]]. He drove the [[Toro Rosso STR10]] on the first day of the test and the [[Red Bull RB11]] on the second day, recording 203 laps in total.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://formulascout.com/gasly-makes-f1-test-debut-among-junior-racers-in-barcelona-action/27699|title=Gasly makes F1 test debut among junior racers in Barcelona action|website=formulascout.com|first=Peter|last=Allen|date=13 May 2015|access-date=24 June 2021}}</ref> He later tested the RB11 again at the [[Red Bull Ring]] in June and was officially named [[Red Bull Racing]]'s reserve driver in September.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.espn.co.uk/f1/story/_/id/13776198/red-bull-names-pierre-gasly-official-formula-one-reserve-driver|title=Pierre Gasly named official Red Bull reserve driver|work=ESPN|access-date=10 October 2015}}</ref><ref name="RB Testing">{{cite news|url=http://www.redbullracing.com/athlete/pierre-gasly#|title=ABOUT PIERRE|publisher=Red Bull Racing|access-date=9 March 2017|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170221012929/http://www.redbullracing.com/athlete/pierre-gasly|archive-date=21 February 2017}}</ref> Gasly continued testing for Red Bull and Toro Rosso during the {{F1|2016}} and {{F1|2017}} seasons.<ref name="RB Testing"/>

=== Toro Rosso (2017–2018) ===
==== 2017 ====
[[File:Pierre Gasly 2017 Malaysia FP1.jpg|thumb|Gasly on his debut for Toro Rosso at the [[2017 Malaysian Grand Prix]]|left]] Gasly made his Formula One race debut at the [[2017 Malaysian Grand Prix]] with [[Toro Rosso]], replacing [[Daniil Kvyat]].<ref name="Toro Rosso">{{Cite news|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/toro-rosso-confirms-gasly-for-malaysian-gp-957830/|title=Toro Rosso confirms Gasly for next races|work=Motorsport.com|date=26 September 2017|access-date=26 September 2017|language=en}}</ref> He finished the Malaysian and [[2017 Japanese Grand Prix|Japanese]] Grands Prix outside the points. Gasly was expected to take Carlos Sainz Jr.'s seat at the {{F1 GP|2017|United States}} after Sainz left for [[Renault in F1|Renault]], but he was forced to miss the race due to a clash with the final round of the [[2017 Super Formula Championship]].<ref>{{cite news|last1=Barretto|first1=Lawrence|title=Gasly to miss Austin F1 race for Super Formula|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/gasly-austin-super-formula-finale-964670/|access-date=12 October 2017|publisher=[[Motorsport.com]]|date=12 October 2017|archive-date=12 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171012202642/https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/gasly-austin-super-formula-finale-964670/|url-status=dead}}</ref> He returned to the team for the {{F1 GP|2017|Mexican}}, partnering with [[Brendon Hartley]] after the team decided to drop Kvyat from the Red Bull programme.

==== 2018 ====
[[File:Pierre Gasly 2018 Azerbaijan GP.jpg|alt=|thumb|Gasly at the [[2018 Azerbaijan Grand Prix]]]]
Gasly and Hartley became full-time Toro Rosso drivers for the {{F1|2018}} season. At the {{F1 GP|2018|Bahrain}}, Gasly qualified sixth but was promoted to fifth on the grid after a penalty for [[Lewis Hamilton]]. He finished the race in fourth place after [[Kimi Räikkönen]] retired, earning his first points finish in Formula One.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/135276/marko-gasly-fourth-made-a-man-out-of-him|title=Marko: Toro Rosso Bahrain GP result made a man out of Pierre Gasly|last=Sharaf|first=Pablo Elizalde, Dominik|work=Autosport.com|access-date=20 April 2018|language=en}}</ref> A week later in [[2018 Chinese Grand Prix|China]], he crashed into teammate Hartley in what the two confessed as being a 'miscommunication'.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/11332315/pierre-gasly-and-brendon-hartley-say-miscommunication-caused-chinese-gp-collision|title=Pierre Gasly and Brendon Hartley say 'miscommunication' caused Chinese GP collision|work=Sky Sports|access-date=20 April 2018|language=en}}</ref> Gasly recorded four more points finishes during the season, including seventh place at [[2018 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco]] and sixth place at [[2018 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungary]]. He ended the season in fifteenth place in the championship with 29 points, comfortably ahead of Hartley's total of four points.

=== Red Bull (2019) ===
==== 2019 ====
[[File:Pierre Gasly during Hungarian Formula 1 GP.jpg|thumb|left|Gasly at the [[2019 Hungarian Grand Prix]]]]
Gasly was contracted to drive for [[Red Bull Racing]] for the {{F1|2019}} season, partnering [[Max Verstappen]] following the departure of [[Daniel Ricciardo]] to Renault.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/8/gasly-to-partner-verstappen-at-red-bull-in-2019.html |title=Gasly to partner Verstappen at Red Bull in 2019 |work=www.formula1.com |date=20 August 2018 |access-date=20 August 2018 |archive-date=25 March 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190325141948/https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/headlines/2018/8/gasly-to-partner-verstappen-at-red-bull-in-2019.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.renaultsport.com/daniel-ricciardo-joins-renault-sport-formula-one-team-from-2019.html?r=331&lang=en|title=Daniel Ricciardo joins Renault Sport Formula One Team from 2019|website=renaultsport.com|access-date=4 August 2018|archive-date=11 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200711045459/https://www.renaultsport.com/daniel-ricciardo-joins-renault-sport-formula-one-team-from-2019.html?r=331&lang=en|url-status=dead}}</ref> Gasly qualified seventeenth at his first race with the team, the {{F1 GP|2019|Australian}} and failed to score in the race. He again failed to qualify in the top ten at the following {{F1 GP|2019|Bahrain}}, and retired from sixth place with a driveshaft issue at the {{F1 GP|2019|Azerbaijan}}. He scored points at the following six races but finished a lap behind the leaders at the [[2019 Canadian Grand Prix|Canadian]], [[2019 French Grand Prix|French]] and [[2019 Austrian Grand Prix|Austrian]] Grands Prix, the latter of which was won by Verstappen.<ref name="Motorsport 2019">{{cite web|url=https://www.motorsport.com/f1/news/top-stories-of-2019-13-gasly-albon-mid-season-swap/4614235/|title=Top Stories of 2019, #13: Gasly loses Red Bull seat to Albon|website=motorsport.com|first=Pablo|last=Elizalde|date=19 December 2019|access-date=24 June 2021}}</ref> His best result with Red Bull Racing came at the {{F1 GP|2019|British}} where he finished fourth after Verstappen and [[Sebastian Vettel]] collided ahead. At the {{F1 GP|2019|German}}, Gasly retired after colliding with [[Alex Albon]]'s Toro Rosso in an attempt to overtake for sixth place.

Gasly came under increasing pressure at the {{F1 GP|2019|Hungarian}}, where he finished sixth having been lapped by Verstappen. Despite Gasly's poor results, Red Bull team principal [[Christian Horner]] and advisor [[Helmut Marko]] stated that the team intended to keep Gasly until the end of the season.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.analysis-why-red-bull-made-their-latest-blockbuster-driver-swap.5OSpVBQ1sdbxO5VEj80ykZ.html|title=ANALYSIS: Why Red Bull made their latest blockbuster driver swap|website=formula1.com|date=12 August 2019|access-date=5 January 2020}}</ref><ref name="Motorsport 2019"/> At this stage of the season, Gasly was sixth in the drivers' championship with 63 points. Verstappen, meanwhile, had recorded 181 points, two race wins, five podium finishes, and one pole position.

=== Return to Toro Rosso / AlphaTauri (2019–2022) ===
[[File:Pierre Gasly, Toro Rosso-Honda STR14, 2019 Italian Grand Prix, Monza, 6th September.jpg|thumb|Gasly at the [[2019 Italian Grand Prix]], after being dropped to [[Scuderia Toro Rosso]] mid-season|left]]
[[File:Fórmula 1 – Grande Prêmio do Brasil de F1 2019 (49080039668).jpg|alt=|thumb|Gasly (second from left) on his maiden podium after finishing second in the [[2019 Brazilian Grand Prix]]]]Ahead of the {{F1 GP|2019|Belgian}}, Gasly was demoted back to junior team Toro Rosso with Alex Albon taking his place at Red Bull.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.albon-to-replace-gasly-at-red-bull-from-belgium.5f9BkwxuJ5TF86EPKqqwJa.html|title=Albon to replace Gasly at Red Bull from Belgium|website=www.formula1.com|language=en|access-date=12 August 2019}}</ref> Red Bull stated that the swap was made in order to "make an informed decision as to who will drive alongside Max [Verstappen] in 2020",<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/news/f1/red-bull-drops-pierre-gasly-alexander-albon|title=Red Bull drops Pierre Gasly for Alexander Albon|date=12 August 2019|website=Motor Sport Magazine|language=en|access-date=12 August 2019}} {{verify source |date=September 2019 |reason=This ref was deleted Special:Diff/910490345 by a bug in VisualEditor and later restored by a bot from the original cite located at Special:Permalink/910487172 cite #20 - verify the cite is accurate and delete this template. [[User:GreenC bot/Job 18]]}}</ref> with Horner remarking that "we desperately need [Gasly] realising more of the potential of the car."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/49317809|title=Red Bull: Alexander Albon to replace Pierre Gasly|date=12 August 2019|access-date=12 August 2019}}</ref>

During the remainder of the season at Toro Rosso, partnering with [[Daniil Kvyat]], Gasly achieved five points finishes, including ninth place at his first race back at the team at the {{F1 GP|2019|Belgian}}. His best result came at the {{F1 GP|2019|Brazilian}}, where he qualified in seventh place and took advantage of retirements from [[Valtteri Bottas]] and both [[Scuderia Ferrari|Ferrari]] drivers, as well as a collision between Lewis Hamilton and Alex Albon, to finish the race in second place after holding off Hamilton in a straight drag to the finish line. This marked the first podium finish of Gasly's Formula One career, Toro Rosso's best race result since the [[2008 Italian Grand Prix]], and [[Honda in Formula One|Honda]]'s first 1–2 finish since the [[1991 Japanese Grand Prix]]. On his cool-down lap, Gasly remarked over the radio: "This is the best day of my life".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.this-is-the-best-day-of-my-life-says-gasly-after-stunning-brazil-podium.1zPKbxMsbW7H1YKoRs5JsK.html|title='This is the best day of my life' says Gasly after stunning Brazil podium|website=formula1.com|date=17 November 2019|access-date=5 January 2020}}</ref> He ended the season seventh in the drivers' championship with 95 points; during his nine races back at Toro Rosso, he scored 32 points compared to 10 for teammate Kvyat in the same period.

==== 2020: Maiden win ====
[[File:Pierre Gasly-Alpha Tauri AT01 (2) (cropped).jpg|left|thumb|Gasly at [[2020 Formula One pre-season testing|pre-season testing in 2020]] ]]
Gasly was retained by the team, along with Kvyat, as they rebranded to [[Scuderia AlphaTauri]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=30 November 2019|title=Toro Rosso's name change to AlphaTauri confirmed in provisional 2020 entry list|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.toro-rossos-name-change-to-alpha-tauri-confirmed-in-provisional-2020-entry.5tIxtPAMsY8MACQAyvg41j.html|access-date=9 September 2020|website=formula1.com}}</ref> Gasly achieved four points finishes in the first seven races of the season, with best results of seventh place at the [[2020 Austrian Grand Prix|Austrian]] and [[2020 British Grand Prix|British]] Grands Prix.

At the {{F1 GP|2020|Italian}}, an early pit stop allowed Gasly to pass several drivers - who had to wait for the pitlane to open during a safety car procedure - promoting him to third place. As race leader Hamilton entered the pits to serve a penalty and second-placed driver [[Lance Stroll]] lost places at the restart, Gasly inherited the lead of the race and held off the late-charging Carlos Sainz Jr. to take his first win in Formula One,<ref>{{cite news |title=Pierre Gasly holds off Carlos Sainz to take an astonishing win in chaotic and thrilling Italian Grand Prix |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/formula-1/2020/09/06/f1-italian-grand-prix-2020-live-monza-race-results-hamilton1/ |website=The Telegraph |date=6 September 2020 |access-date=6 September 2020|last1=Duncan |first1=Phil |last2=Slater |first2=Luke }}</ref> becoming the 109th different race winner and the first French driver to win a Grand Prix since [[Olivier Panis]]' victory at the [[1996 Monaco Grand Prix]], 24 years prior.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/12065282/italian-gp-pierre-gasly-wins-for-alphatauri-after-lewis-hamilton-penalty|title=Italian GP: Pierre Gasly wins for AlphaTauri after Lewis Hamilton penalty|website=Sky Sports|date=6 September 2020|access-date=6 September 2020}}</ref> This prompted speculation that Gasly could return to Red Bull, as his replacement, Alex Albon, finished only fifteenth. Gasly remarked that he was 'ready' to do so.<ref>{{Cite news|date=7 September 2020|url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/pierre-gasly-ready-red-bull/|website=www.planetf1.com|title=Gasly 'ready' for second Red Bull chance}}</ref> AlphaTauri team principal [[Franz Tost]] praised Gasly's race but dismissed the possibility of Gasly returning to Red Bull in the near future.<ref>{{Cite news|title=Tost praises 'fantastic' race from Gasly - but scotches talk of imminent Red Bull return|date=7 September 2020|website=www.formula1.com|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.tost-praises-fantastic-race-from-gasly-but-scotches-talk-of-imminent-red.7cWEuQj0gDAmFI4eC49aO.html}}</ref>

Gasly was eliminated in a first-lap collision at the following race, the {{F1 GP|2020|Tuscan}}. He scored points at the next three races, including fifth place at the {{F1 GP|2020|Portuguese}}, but retired with a coolant leak at the {{F1 GP|2020|Emilia Romagna}} having qualified a season-best fourth. He finished the 2020 season tenth in the drivers' championship with 75 points, ahead of teammate Kvyat's 32 points.

==== 2021 ====
[[File:FIA F1 Austria 2021 Nr. 10 Gasly.jpg|thumb|Gasly at the [[2021 Austrian Grand Prix]]]]
Gasly was retained by AlphaTauri for {{F1|2021}},<ref>{{Cite web|date=28 October 2020|title=AlphaTauri confirm Pierre Gasly is to remain with the team for 2021|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.breaking-alphatauri-confirm-pierre-gasly-is-to-remain-with-the-team-for-2021.24phzzreYMNPYp8SQ3zTC1.html|access-date=28 October 2020|website=Formula1.com}}</ref> partnered by Japanese rookie [[Yuki Tsunoda]]. He qualified fifth for the season-opening {{F1 GP|2021|Bahrain}} but collided with Daniel Ricciardo and later retired from the race. He then scored points at the following six races, including his third career podium at the {{F1 GP|2021|Azerbaijan}}. He qualified fourth, dropped to fifth early in the race, but benefited from a tyre failure for Max Verstappen and a mistake from Lewis Hamilton in the closing stages and prevailed in a battle with [[Charles Leclerc]] on the penultimate lap to finish third.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2021 Azerbaijan Grand Prix race report and highlights: Perez beats Vettel to Baku victory after Verstappen crashes out from lead late on|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.perez-beats-vettel-to-baku-victory-after-verstappen-crashes-out-from-lead.aLIxuNGxwBFgZ4Z8OhuRw.html|access-date=6 June 2021|website=www.formula1.com|language=en}}</ref> Gasly's points streak ended at the {{F1 GP|2021|Styrian}} when he was eliminated by a first-lap collision with Leclerc. A late-race puncture at the {{F1 GP|2021|British}} dropped Gasly out of the points positions.<ref name="Motor Sport magazine what you missed">{{Cite news |url= https://www.motorsportmagazine.com/articles/single-seaters/f1/2021-british-grand-prix-what-you-missed |title=2021 British Grand Prix: what you missed |work=[[Motor Sport magazine|MotorSport]] |last=Williams-Smith |first=Jake |date=19 July 2021 |access-date=20 July 2021}}</ref>

At the {{F1 GP|2021|Italian}}, Gasly crashed out in sprint qualifying after he made contact with Daniel Ricciardo, wedging his front wing underneath his wheels. He retired from the race on lap four due to damaged suspension.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.redbull.com/int-en/italian-f1-grand-prix-2021-race-report|title=Max Verstappen extends advantage after incident-packed Italian Grand Prix|website=redbull.com|date=12 September 2021 |access-date=18 September 2021}}</ref> He finished sixth at the {{F1 GP|2021|Turkish}} despite a penalty for causing a collision with [[Fernando Alonso]]. Gasly started on the front row for the first time at the {{F1 GP|2021|Qatar}} but finished the race outside the points.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.alpine-did-a-better-job-than-us-says-gasly-as-front-row-start-goes.5R7o8hgOtRIU3kysivgrYv.html|title=Alpine 'did a better job than us' says Gasly, as front-row start goes unrewarded in Qatar|website=formula1.com|access-date=18 March 2022}}</ref> Gasly finished the season ninth in the drivers' standings with his highest ever points tally in a single season, scoring 110 points to teammate Tsunoda's 32.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.gasly-calls-2021-most-consistent-year-in-f1-after-netting-highest-ever.77IehlGdBcuU4hQnodn998.html|title=Gasly calls 2021 'most consistent' year in F1 after netting highest ever points tally|website=formula1.com|access-date=18 March 2022}}</ref>

==== 2022 ====
[[File:Pierre Gasly 2022 Emilia Romagna GP.jpg|thumb|left|Gasly at the [[2022 Emilia Romagna Grand Prix]]]]
Gasly continued to race for AlphaTauri in {{F1|2022}} alongside Tsunoda.<ref>{{Cite web|date=7 September 2021|title=Pierre Gasly and Yuki Tsunoda retained by AlphaTauri for 2022|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.pierre-gasly-and-yuki-tsunoda-retained-by-alphatauri-for-2022.7r4sLtzK5gjpiek9848EWh.html|access-date=7 September 2021|website=Formula1}}</ref> An engine fire caused his retirement from the season-opening {{F1 GP|2022|Bahrain}}. He scored points at the [[2022 Saudi Arabian Grand Prix|Saudi Arabian]] and [[2022 Australian Grand Prix|Australian]] Grands Prix, but failed to do so at the following four races, which included retirement from a collision with [[Lando Norris]] at the {{F1 GP|2022|Miami}}. He ended the zero-point streak by finishing fifth at the {{F1 GP|2022|Azerbaijan}}.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.sportskeeda.com/f1/news-we-made-mistakes-weekend-pierre-gasly-says-fighting-mercedes-2022-f1-azerbaijan-gp-means-alphatauri-a-really-good-job | title="We made no mistakes this weekend" - Pierre Gasly says fighting with Mercedes at 2022 F1 Azerbaijan GP means AlphaTauri did 'a really good job' | date=13 June 2022 }}</ref> Five more races without points followed, including a collision with teammate Tsunoda that ended his race at the {{F1 GP|2022|British}}. Gasly qualified tenth at the {{F1 GP|2022|Austrian}} but collided with Lewis Hamilton in the sprint and Sebastian Vettel in the race, finishing fifteenth.

Gasly was set to start the {{F1 GP|2022|Belgian}} from eighth place, however an electrical issue shortly before the race forced him to start from the pit lane. He recovered to the points positions, finishing ninth.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://racingnews365.com/electrical-problem-nearly-stopped-gasly-going-from-pitlane-to-points|title=Gasly reveals technical issues which almost prevented Belgian GP points|website=racingnews365|first=Rory|last=Mitchell|date=28 August 2022|access-date=1 September 2022}}</ref> His final points of the season came at the [[2022 Italian Grand Prix|Italian]] and [[2022 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]] Grands Prix. Gasly had run in seventh place in Singapore, but complained that the team "threw away" this result by switching him to dry-weather tyres too early, dropping him to tenth.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/upset-gasly-says-alphatauri-threw-away-singapore-gp/10378671/|title=Upset Gasly says AlphaTauri "threw away" F1 Singapore GP|website=autosport.com|first1=Jonathan|last1=Noble|first2=Oleg|last2=Karpov|date=3 October 2022|access-date=24 December 2022}}</ref> At the {{F1 GP|2022|Japanese}}, Gasly criticised the deployment of a recovery tractor in wet conditions to extract Carlos Sainz Jr.'s crashed car, describing it as "disrespectful" to the memory of the late [[Jules Bianchi]] and stating "I could have killed myself".<ref>{{Cite news |title=Anger as truck triggers memories of Bianchi death |language=en-GB |work=BBC Sport |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/formula1/63190448 |access-date=9 October 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Takle |first=Abhishek |date=9 October 2022 |title='I could have killed myself': Gasly fumes over tractor near-miss |url=https://www.smh.com.au/sport/motorsport/i-could-have-killed-myself-gasly-fumes-over-tractor-near-miss-20221009-p5boe2.html |access-date=9 October 2022 |website=The Sydney Morning Herald |language=en}}</ref> He received a penalty for speeding under the subsequent [[Racing flags#Red flag|red flag]] and was demoted to eighteenth place. Gasly ended the season fourteenth in the drivers' championship, scoring 23 points to Tsunoda's twelve.

After the [[2022 Azerbaijan Grand Prix|Azerbaijan Grand Prix]] in June, AlphaTauri team principal [[Franz Tost]] confirmed that Gasly had a contract with the team and would remain with them for {{F1|2023}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Pierre Gasly will be Alpha Tauri driver in 2023, says team principal Franz Tost |url=https://www.skysports.com/watch/video/sports/f1/12636326/franz-tost-gasly-will-be-alpha-tauri-driver-in-2023 |access-date=18 June 2022 |website=SkySports |language=en}}</ref> In August and September, information emerged that [[Alpine F1 Team|Alpine]] were targeting Gasly after [[Fernando Alonso]] and [[Oscar Piastri]] both left the team, and that Red Bull were willing to release him.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Woodhouse |first=Jamie |date=29 August 2022 |title=Alpine target Pierre Gasly with Oscar Piastri no longer preferred option - report |url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/pierre-gasly-alpine-preferred-option/ |access-date=4 September 2022 |website=PlanetF1 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Foster |first=Michelle |date=2 September 2022 |title=Path reportedly cleared for Pierre Gasly to move to Alpine with terms agreed |url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/alpine-red-bull-agree-pierre-gasly-terms/ |access-date=4 September 2022 |website=PlanetF1 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Cooper |first=Sam |date=2 September 2022 |title=Helmut Marko hints Pierre Gasly is on the verge of joining Alpine from AlphaTauri |url=https://www.planetf1.com/news/helmut-marko-pierre-gasly-alpine/ |access-date=4 September 2022 |website=PlanetF1 |language=en}}</ref> Gasly's move to Alpine for 2023 was officially announced in October.<ref name=":1" />

=== Alpine (2023–present) ===
==== 2023 ====
[[File:FIA F1 Austria 2023 Nr. 10 (1).jpg|thumb|Gasly driving his Alpine A523 during the 2023 Austrian Grand Prix]]
Gasly signed a multi-year contract worth in excess of $10 million a year to race for [[Alpine F1 Team|Alpine]] in [[2023 Formula One World Championship|2023]], partnering fellow Frenchman and former karting rival [[Esteban Ocon]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=8 October 2022 |title=Pierre Gasly completes 2023 BWT Alpine F1 Team driver line up |url=https://media.alpinecars.com/pierre-gasly-completes-2023-bwt-alpine-f1-team-driver-line-up/ |access-date=8 October 2022 |website=Newsroom Alpine}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Kalinauckas |first=Alex |date=2026-05-03 |title=Pierre Gasly’s desperate trip to Uganda to fund an F1 dream: ‘I’m not even sure he exists’ |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7215965/2026/04/23/pierre-gasly-uganda-sponsor-f1/ |access-date=2026-05-08 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> AlphaTauri allowed Gasly to join Alpine for the post-season testing immediately after the [[2022 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Piastri and Gasly to run for McLaren and Alpine at post-season Abu Dhabi test following agreement {{!}} Formula 1® |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.piastri-and-gasly-to-run-for-mclaren-and-alpine-at-post-season-abu-dhabi.1lyuikHNHX28HiY7AvKcRh.html |access-date=13 November 2022 |website=www.formula1.com |language=en}}</ref>

In his first race with Alpine at the {{F1 GP|2023|Bahrain}}, Gasly started last after having his qualifying time deleted, but recovered to finish ninth in the race.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.verstappen-leads-1-2-in-bahrain-season-opener-as-leclerc-retires-and-alonso.40788dU0VkiLz0gxRPo7n1.html|title=Verstappen leads 1-2 in Bahrain season opener as Leclerc retires and Alonso takes final podium place in style|work=Formula 1|date=5 March 2023|access-date=27 April 2023}}</ref> Gasly was in fifth place when racing resumed after the second red flag at the {{F1 GP|2023|Australian}}, but collided with Ocon at the first [[chicane]], causing the retirement of both Alpine cars. His practice running at the {{F1 GP|2023|Azerbaijan}} was curtailed by an engine fire.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://racingnews365.com/dramatic-gasly-fire-triggers-critical-red-flag|title=Dramatic Gasly fire triggers critical red flag|first=Ewan|last=Gale|website=RacingNews365.com|date=28 April 2023}}</ref> He later crashed in qualifying and failed to score points in the sprint or the main race. Gasly qualified in the top ten and scored points at the following three races; this included starting fifth on the grid at the {{F1 GP|2023|Miami}} and qualifying fourth at the {{F1 GP|2023|Spanish}}, before being demoted by two separate penalties for impeding.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/gasly-handed-two-grid-penalties-for-impeding-in-barcelona-f1-qualifying/10478158/|title=Gasly handed two grid penalties for impeding in Barcelona F1 qualifying|last=Kew|first=Matt|website=Autosport.com|date=4 June 2023}}</ref>

Gasly next scored a point at the {{F1 GP|2023|Austrian}}, where he finished ninth but was dropped one position for track limits infringements.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2023/races/1213/austria/race-result.html|title=Formula 1 Rolex Großer Preis von Österreich 2023 – Race Result|website=Formula 1|date=2 July 2023|access-date=2 July 2023}}</ref> Two retirements from collisions followed; one with Lance Stroll at the {{F1 GP|2023|British}} and a multi-car accident on the opening lap of the {{F1 GP|2023|Hungarian}}. He qualified sixth for the {{F1 GP|2023|Belgian}} sprint and an early switch to [[intermediate tyre]]s promoted him to third place, earning six points. He did not score in the main race, where he finished eleventh. His sprint podium was followed by a Grand Prix podium at the {{F1 GP|2023|Dutch}}; he started twelfth and again benefited from an early stop for intermediate tyres. He crossed the finish line fourth but gained a place from [[Sergio Pérez]]'s penalty, earning his first podium in over two years. Gasly achieved four more points finishes before the end of the season, including sixth-place finishes at the [[2023 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]] and [[2023 United States Grand Prix|United States]] Grands Prix. He started fourth at the {{F1 GP|2023|Las Vegas}} but finished outside the points.

Gasly ended his first season with Alpine eleventh in the World Drivers' Championship with 62 points, one place and four points ahead of teammate Ocon.

==== 2024 ====
[[File:Pierre Gasly 2024 Chinese GP.jpg|thumb|left|Gasly at the [[2024 Chinese Grand Prix]]]]
Gasly and Ocon remained at Alpine for {{F1|2024}}. They qualified in the bottom two positions for the {{F1 GP|2024|Bahrain}}. Ocon had commented after pre-season testing that the team were "on the back foot" and Gasly described the Bahrain result as "not a surprise".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.alpine-drivers-admit-team-on-the-back-foot-heading-into-2024.3ZeFMgcILBn1aSGZ7Bi60C.html|title=Alpine drivers admit team 'on the back foot' heading into 2024|website=formula1.com|date=27 February 2024|accessdate=16 March 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article.we-have-a-lot-of-work-ahead-of-us-alpine-drivers-say-double-q1-exit-not-a.5EFWYAdo9eZoruH8TDlaPA.html|title='We have a lot of work ahead of us' – Alpine drivers say double-Q1 exit 'not a surprise' in Bahrain|website=formula1.com|date=1 March 2024|accessdate=16 March 2024}}</ref> Both drivers were again eliminated in the first qualifying session (Q1) at the {{F1 GP|2024|Saudi Arabian}} and Gasly was forced into retirement on the first lap of the race with gearbox issues. Gasly finished no higher than 12th position at the [[2024 Australian Grand Prix|Australian]], [[2024 Japanese Grand Prix|Japanese]], [[2024 Chinese Grand Prix|Chinese]], [[2024 Miami Grand Prix|Miami]], and [[2024 Emilia Romagna Grand Prix|Emilia Romagna]] Grands Prix, failing to score points in any of those races.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.formula1.com/en/results.html/2024/drivers/PIEGAS01/pierre-gasly.html | title=F1 - the Official Home of Formula 1® Racing }}</ref> Gasly's teammate, Esteban Ocon, ran upgrades to the Alpine car during the Chinese weekend but Gasly did not, receiving the same upgrades at the Miami Grand Prix.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.essentiallysports.com/f1-news-chinese-gp-2024-pierre-gasly-compromised-by-alpine-while-esteban-ocon-enters-the-top-10-with-upgrades/ | title=Chinese GP 2024: Pierre Gasly "Compromised" by Alpine While Esteban Ocon Enters the Top 10 with Upgrades | date=19 April 2024 }}</ref> He scored his first point of the season at the [[2024 Monaco Grand Prix|Monaco Grand Prix]], coming in tenth position, and two points at the [[2024 Canadian Grand Prix|Canadian Grand Prix]], coming in ninth. Gasly then went on to qualify in seventh for the [[2024 Spanish Grand Prix|Spanish Grand Prix]], before finishing ninth in the main race, whilst grabbing another point at the [[2024 Austrian Grand Prix|Austrian Grand Prix]]. Gasly retired in the next two races at the [[2024 British Grand Prix|British Grand Prix]] and [[2024 Hungarian Grand Prix|Hungarian Grand Prix]]. Gasly bounced back at the [[2024 Dutch Grand Prix|Dutch Grand Prix]] with a ninth place and two points, before enduring a four race pointless streak across the [[2024 Italian Grand Prix|Italian]], [[2024 Azerbaijan Grand Prix|Azerbaijan]], [[2024 Singapore Grand Prix|Singapore]] and [[2024 United States Grand Prix|United States]] Grands Prix, qualifying sixth at the latter. He ended this run by scoring one point at the {{F1GP|2024|Mexico City}} by finishing tenth. He had a high-scoring weekend at the {{F1GP|2024|São Paulo}}, finishing seventh in the sprint, and third in a rain-affected Grand Prix after starting thirteenth, marking Gasly's fifth career podium and his first since the [[2023 Dutch Grand Prix]]—the result promoted him from seventeenth to twelfth in the standings. He then qualified a career-best third for the {{F1GP|2024|Las Vegas}}, but retired from the race with a mechanical issue in his third retirement of the season. Gasly ended the season on a high however, as he finished 5th in [[2024 Qatar Grand Prix|Qatar]] after holding off [[Carlos Sainz Jr.|Carlos Sainz]] in the latter stages, and seventh in [[2024 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix|Abu Dhabi]]. This would help Alpine beat Haas to sixth in the Constructors' Championship and Gasly to finish tenth in the Drivers' Championship, as he scored 34 points in the final six races of the campaign.

==== 2025 ====
{{Update|section|date=April 2026}}
[[File:2025 Japan GP - Alpine - Pierre Gasly - FP2.jpg|thumb|right|Gasly (pictured at the {{F1GP|2025|Japanese}}) began the season by partnering [[Jack Doohan]] in {{F1|2025}}.|alt=Gasly driving the Alpine A525 at the 2025 Japanese Grand Prix]]
In June 2024, Gasly extended his multi-year contract with Alpine until at least the end of 2026,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Edmondson |first=Laurence |date=27 June 2024 |title=Pierre Gasly signs new long-term contract with Alpine |url=https://www.espn.com/f1/story/_/id/40442864/pierre-gasly-signs-new-long-term-contract-alpine |access-date=27 June 2024 |website=ESPN}}</ref> later extending it to 2028.<ref name="contract">{{Cite web |title=Gasly commits long-term future to Alpine as he signs new contract extension |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/gasly-commits-long-term-future-to-alpine-as-he-signs-new-contract-extension.wgdUGA4Xzo6fWpo6JK7eg |access-date=6 September 2025|website=Formula 1.com|language=en}}</ref> He would be partnering rookie [[Jack Doohan]] in place of the [[Haas F1 Team|Haas]]-bound Ocon.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fiaformula2.com/Latest/1ri7cYSWe9zHXp6VuUKMZH/jack-doohan-confirmed-as-alpine-f1-team-reserve-driver|title=Jack Doohan confirmed as Alpine F1 Team Reserve Driver|website=FIA Formula 2|date=16 February 2023}}</ref> Doohan was replaced by [[Franco Colapinto]] following the [[2025 Miami Grand Prix|Miami Grand Prix]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/alpine-confirm-colapinto-to-replace-doohan-for-next-five-rounds-as-team-opt.4lH96VnGHyC45S3yjeJOMK |title=Alpine confirm Colapinto to replace Doohan for next five rounds as team opt to 'rotate' seat |website=Formula One |date=7 May 2025}}</ref> He was disqualified from the {{F1GP|2025|Chinese}} after his [[Alpine A525|A525]] was found to be underweight.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/leclerc-hamilton-and-gasly-disqualified-from-chinese-gp/10706422/ |title=Leclerc, Hamilton and Gasly disqualified from Chinese GP |last=Cleeren |first=Filip |date=23 March 2025 |website=[[Autosport]] |publisher=[[Motorsport Network]] |access-date=23 March 2025 |issn=0269-946X |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250323122436/https://www.autosport.com/f1/news/leclerc-hamilton-and-gasly-disqualified-from-chinese-gp/10706422/ |archive-date=23 March 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> Gasly scored all of Alpine's points for the season, scoring 22 points throughout the 24 rounds.

==== 2026 ====
[[File:2026 Chinese GP - Alpine - Pierre Gasly - Qualifying.jpg|thumb|right|Gasly pictured at the 2026 Chinese Grand Prix driving the first Mercedes powered Alpine.|alt=Gasly driving the A526 at the 2026 Chinese Grand Prix]]
For the 2026 season, he partnered with Colapinto, the latter starting his first full season in Formula One as Alpine started to use Mercedes engines from the new season onwards. At the [[2026 Australian Grand Prix]], he finished 10th, keeping ex-teammate Ocon at bay who setttled for 11th. At the [[2026 Monaco Grand Prix]] he achieved his sixth career podium by finishing in third place, the first since 2024.

==Other racing==
===Super Formula===
After his success in GP2, Gasly joined [[Mugen Motorsports|Team Mugen]], partnering [[Naoki Yamamoto (racing driver)|Naoki Yamamoto]], to drive a Red Bull-sponsored Honda at the [[2017 Super Formula Championship]].<ref>[https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/gasly-confirmed-at-mugen-for-2017-super-formula-season-873224/ Gasly confirmed at Mugen for 2017 Super Formula season] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170831085803/https://www.motorsport.com/super-formula/news/gasly-confirmed-at-mugen-for-2017-super-formula-season-873224/ |date=31 August 2017 }} - Darshan Chokhani, Motorsport.com, 13 February 2017</ref> He won two races in a row before his season was effectively cut short by the cancellation of the final round at [[Suzuka Circuit]] due to [[Typhoon Lan (2017)|Typhoon Lan]]. Gasly finished second in the standings, half a point from clinching the championship.<ref>[https://www.autosport.com/general/news/super-formula-suzuka-finale-cancelled-gasly-loses-title-shot-4993424/4993424/ Super Formula Suzuka finale cancelled, Gasly loses title shot] - Rachit Thukral, Autosport, 21 October 2017</ref>
[[File:Pierre Gasly Formula E car.png|thumb|left|Gasly's Renault e.dams car in the NYC ePrix paddock prior to qualifying]]
===Formula E===
Gasly made a one-off [[Formula E]] appearance for [[Renault {{Not a typo|e.dams}}]] where he replaced [[Sébastien Buemi]] for the [[2017 New York City ePrix|2017 New York ePrix]], due to the latter's commitments to the [[2017 FIA World Endurance Championship|World Endurance Championship]].<ref name="Gasly e.dams">{{cite web|url=http://www.autosport.com/news/report.php/id/130644/gasly-confirmed-as-buemi-new-york-standin|title=Renault e.dams confirms Gasly as Buemi replacement for New York FE|date=10 July 2017|access-date=10 July 2017 |last1=Smith |first1=Sam }}</ref> In the weekend's first race, Gasly recovered from the poor qualifying performance of 19th to finish seventh in his debut race.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://autoweek.com/article/other-motorsports/sam-bird-wins-first-formula-e-new-york-city-eprix|title=Sam Bird wins first Formula E New York City ePrix|work=Autoweek|date=16 July 2017|access-date=18 July 2017}}</ref> Gasly nearly finished on the podium in the second race, hitting the wall on the exit of the final corner while battling for third and limping across the finish line with major damage in fourth.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motorsport.com/formula-e/news/new-york-eprix-bird-completes-brooklyn-sweep-in-sunday-race-931817/|title=New York ePrix: Bird completes Brooklyn sweep in Sunday race|work=Motorsport.com|publisher=Motorsport Network|last=Mitchell|first=Scott|date=16 July 2017|access-date=18 July 2017}}</ref>

